{
  "opening": "This release introduces significant updates to the glom library, including new mutation capabilities for deleting data paths and enhanced support for self-recursive structures via the Ref specification. Additionally, several aggregation specs have been refined to ensure consistent behavior across different modes, and the command-line interface gains improved testing coverage.",
  "requirements": [
    {
      "id": "m04",
      "text": "The public API must expose the Delete spec, the delete function, and the PathDeleteError exception under their exact names. Users should be able to use these components to remove specific keys or indices from nested data structures, with errors raised appropriately when deletion targets invalid paths."
    },
    {
      "id": "m08",
      "text": "The Ref spec must be available as a public name to allow specifications to reference themselves recursively. This enables users to define transformations that operate on arbitrarily deep or cyclic data structures without hardcoding depth limits."
    },
    {
      "id": "m10",
      "text": "Ensure that all existing core specs such as Assign, Call, Check, Coalesce, Fill, First, Flatten, Fold, Invoke, Iter, Let, Literal, Path, Spec, Sum, and UnregisteredTarget remain accessible and functional. The format_invocation and kwarg_fmt utilities must continue to provide consistent formatting for invocation debugging."
    },
    {
      "id": "m12",
      "text": "The command-line interface must expose the get_command, glom_cli, main, and mw_handle_target functions to support CLI execution and target handling. These entries ensure the CLI can correctly parse arguments and process input data formats like JSON or Python objects."
    },
    {
      "id": "m13",
      "text": "The mw_get_target function must be publicly available to facilitate middleware-based target retrieval in CLI contexts. This supports flexible data source loading mechanisms required by the command-line tool."
    },
    {
      "id": "m17",
      "text": "The BadSpec exception and Count spec must be exposed as public names alongside other aggregation specs like Auto, Avg, Fill, First, Fold, Group, Limit, Max, Min, Sample, and Sum. Users should encounter BadSpec when providing invalid specifications, and Count should behave consistently within group modes."
    },
    {
      "id": "m18",
      "text": "The Count spec must function both as a reduction spec and an aggregator, replacing any legacy grouping.Sum behavior. The agg namespace should reflect this unified approach, ensuring Sum and other reduction specs maintain backward compatibility while supporting new aggregation patterns."
    }
  ],
  "closing": "Please verify that all specified public names are correctly exported and that error messages remain clear and actionable. Ensure backward compatibility is maintained for existing users relying on current spec behaviors."
}
